1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,400
The Sisters' Islands and
Singapore's First Marine Park

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,619
Two small wooded islands sit
side by side south of Sentosa,

3
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:12,925
so close together that from
the sea they look like a pair.

4
00:00:12,925 --> 00:00:15,568
A Malay legend says they were once two

5
00:00:15,648 --> 00:00:18,300
sisters who drowned there in a storm.

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,694
In the colonial era soldiers fired
howitzers at targets on them,

7
00:00:22,774 --> 00:00:27,936
in the 1970s they were reclaimed into
picnic islands with swimming lagoons,

8
00:00:28,016 --> 00:00:31,711
and since 2014 they have
been the heart of Singapore's

9
00:00:31,791 --> 00:00:36,125
first marine park, where hawksbill
turtles now nest and hatch.

10
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:40,149
The islands are Pulau Subar
Laut, Big Sister's Island,

11
00:00:40,229 --> 00:00:43,797
which faces the open sea,
and Pulau Subar Darat,

12
00:00:43,877 --> 00:00:47,525
Small Sister's Island,
which faces the mainland.

13
00:00:47,525 --> 00:00:51,475
In Malay, laut means
seaward and darat landward.

14
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:54,311
A narrow but deep channel
runs between them,

15
00:00:54,391 --> 00:00:58,500
with currents strong enough to
make swimming across dangerous.

16
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:02,273
The best-known version of
the legend, as the National

17
00:01:02,353 --> 00:01:06,425
Library tells it, is about
two sisters, Minah and Linah.

18
00:01:06,425 --> 00:01:10,850
A pirate chief seized Linah and
carried her off in his boat.

19
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:14,421
Minah swam after them and
was overcome by a wave,

20
00:01:14,501 --> 00:01:18,450
and Linah threw herself
overboard to join her sister.

21
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:22,675
After a storm, two islands appeared
where the sisters had drowned,

22
00:01:22,755 --> 00:01:25,625
and the villagers named
them after the pair.

23
00:01:25,625 --> 00:01:29,147
Other versions differ in
the details, but in all of

24
00:01:29,227 --> 00:01:32,900
them the sisters end up
together as the two islands.

25
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:37,400
Before the 1970s the islands
were smaller than they are today,

26
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:41,025
and in the colonial era
they had a military use.

27
00:01:41,025 --> 00:01:44,458
In March 1905 the Straits
Times carried a notice

28
00:01:44,538 --> 00:01:47,678
that howitzer practice
would be held "at the

29
00:01:47,758 --> 00:01:51,125
targets on Sisters Island"
the following week.

30
00:01:51,125 --> 00:01:53,920
After independence the Southern Islands

31
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,875
were turned into places for recreation.

32
00:01:56,875 --> 00:02:01,795
In 1974 and 1975, reclamation
added about 5.4 hectares

33
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:06,333
to the two islands, and
seawalls were built along

34
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:10,950
the reef edges to enclose
sandy swimming lagoons.

35
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:16,338
A 1975 report in the Straits Times
described Belgian contractors

36
00:02:16,418 --> 00:02:21,800
helping to reclaim Kusu, the
Sisters, Hantu, Sebarok and Coney.

37
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,983
From 1976 the Sentosa
Development Corporation looked

38
00:02:26,063 --> 00:02:30,739
after the islands, and by the
late 1970s community centres

39
00:02:30,819 --> 00:02:35,000
and youth groups were
running Sunday picnics there.

40
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,966
The reclamation that made the beaches also

41
00:02:38,046 --> 00:02:40,875
buried much of the islands' reef flats.

42
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:44,681
Across Singapore, the reefs
suffered far more from decades

43
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:49,505
of land reclamation, dredging of
shipping channels and dumping of earth,

44
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:52,600
which clouded the water
and smothered corals.

45
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:56,818
Marine scientists at the
National University of

46
00:02:56,898 --> 00:03:01,115
Singapore estimate that
the country's reef area

47
00:03:01,195 --> 00:03:05,139
shrank from about 39.85
square kilometres in

48
00:03:05,219 --> 00:03:09,425
1953 to about 13.25
square kilometres in 2012.

49
00:03:09,425 --> 00:03:13,348
Underwater visibility,
which was about 10 metres

50
00:03:13,428 --> 00:03:16,851
in the early 1960s, fell to 2 or 3 metres,

51
00:03:16,931 --> 00:03:20,600
and corals now grow
only in shallower water.

52
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,801
Most of the reefs that survive
fringe the Southern Islands,

53
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:26,550
among them the Sisters.

54
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:30,063
In 2009, a group of marine
scientists and nature

55
00:03:30,143 --> 00:03:34,329
enthusiasts put forward a Blue
Plan for Singapore's seas,

56
00:03:34,409 --> 00:03:38,675
which called for protected
areas for the remaining reefs.

57
00:03:38,675 --> 00:03:42,833
On the 12th of July 2014, at
the Festival of Biodiversity,

58
00:03:42,913 --> 00:03:47,217
the Minister of State for
National Development, Desmond Lee,

59
00:03:47,297 --> 00:03:51,900
announced that Singapore would
establish its first marine park.

60
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,958
The Sisters' Islands Marine
Park covers about 40 hectares:

61
00:03:56,038 --> 00:03:59,025
the two islands and
the waters around them,

62
00:03:59,105 --> 00:04:03,600
and the western reefs of Saint
John's Island and Pulau Tekukor.

63
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:07,667
Free guided walks on the
islands' intertidal shores

64
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:12,058
began in August 2014, a
public gallery on Saint John's

65
00:04:12,138 --> 00:04:15,960
Island opened in July
2015, and two dive trails,

66
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:20,675
a shallow one and a deeper
one, opened in September 2015.

67
00:04:20,675 --> 00:04:25,075
The park has become a base
for research and restoration.

68
00:04:25,075 --> 00:04:27,462
In 2011 a Neptune's cup sponge,

69
00:04:27,542 --> 00:04:32,636
a giant goblet-shaped sponge
thought to have died out after 1908,

70
00:04:32,716 --> 00:04:35,900
was found again off Saint John's Island.

71
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:39,353
In 2018 NParks and JTC
began lowering eight

72
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:43,790
artificial reef structures,
each about 10 metres tall,

73
00:04:43,870 --> 00:04:47,650
into the park's waters,
for corals to grow on.

74
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:50,946
In 2018 NParks opened Singapore's first

75
00:04:51,026 --> 00:04:54,575
turtle hatchery on Small Sister's Island.

76
00:04:54,575 --> 00:04:57,827
Eggs from nests at risk,
laid too close to the

77
00:04:57,907 --> 00:05:01,015
waterline or in busy
places, are moved there

78
00:05:01,095 --> 00:05:04,500
into buried cages and
watched until they hatch.

79
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:07,948
Hawksbill turtles, which
are critically endangered,

80
00:05:08,028 --> 00:05:12,800
nest on several of Singapore's
shores, including Big Sister's Island.

81
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:16,421
In the 2026 season, by
the middle of August,

82
00:05:16,501 --> 00:05:20,123
25 nests had been
recorded across Singapore,

83
00:05:20,203 --> 00:05:24,325
and 294 hatchlings had
emerged from the hatchery.

84
00:05:24,325 --> 00:05:28,190
In 2021 the islands closed for rebuilding,

85
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:34,000
and Big Sister's Island reopened
on the 28th of October 2024.

86
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,538
It now has a floating
boardwalk that rises and falls

87
00:05:37,618 --> 00:05:40,878
with the tide, with coral
panels and moon pools,

88
00:05:40,958 --> 00:05:44,427
a lagoon turned into a
tidal pool, a coastal forest

89
00:05:44,507 --> 00:05:48,125
trail and a view deck on
the island's highest point.

90
00:05:48,125 --> 00:05:52,325
Solar panels power a
small desalination plant.

91
00:05:52,325 --> 00:05:55,390
Small Sister's Island stays closed to the

92
00:05:55,470 --> 00:05:59,075
public as a zone for
conservation and research.

93
00:05:59,075 --> 00:06:03,878
Visitors reach Big Sister's Island
by ferry from Marina South Pier,

94
00:06:03,958 --> 00:06:08,550
a trip of about half an hour that
stops at Saint John's Island.

95
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:12,263
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Sisters' Islands

96
00:06:12,343 --> 00:06:17,022
have been remade several times, from
a legend to an artillery target,

97
00:06:17,102 --> 00:06:20,275
a reclaimed picnic spot
and now a marine park.

98
00:06:20,275 --> 00:06:23,917
Their story mirrors that of
Singapore's reefs as a whole,

99
00:06:23,997 --> 00:06:28,423
which lost most of their area to
the growth of the city and the port,

100
00:06:28,503 --> 00:06:33,400
and the park is one of the ways the
country is trying to keep what remains.
